// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// In-world panels. Fires whatever row the pointing hand is on -- the
// panel system's single "confirm", not a per-action bind.
//
// THIS BIND IS WHY THE FEATURE IS REACHABLE AT ALL. Aiming resolves a
// panel and a row, and the row carries a netevent name, but without
// something to fire it the whole interaction is console-only.
//
// *** THE DEFAULT KEY IS NOT A FREE CHOICE. READ BEFORE CHANGING IT. ***
// This line said MOUSE2 and bound NOTHING. `defaultbind` is not "set
// this key"; FKeyBindings::DefaultBind (c_bind.cpp:531) refuses on two
// conditions -- the key is already bound (:539), or the command is
// already bound somewhere (:543). MOUSE2 is `+altattack` in the
// engine's own defbinds.txt, and KEYCONF is parsed at d_main.cpp:3462,
// AFTER key setup at :3338. So the key was always taken by the time
// this ran, the bind was dropped, and there was no error and no log
// line -- the same inert-failure shape as an unresolved sound name.
//
// MOUSE3 is unbound in both defbinds.txt and commonbinds.txt, so this
// one actually takes. Any replacement must be checked against those two
// lists or it will silently do nothing again.
//
// This is the FLAT-PLAY route and the universal fallback. In VR the
// primary confirm is the weapon trigger itself, captured in
// VR_DualClassBase.PlayerThink -- no bind, and it does not fire the
// gun. See RS_PanelInput.zs.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
addmenukey "In-World Panel: Select Row" rs_panel_use
alias rs_panel_use "netevent rs-panel-use"
defaultbind MOUSE3 rs_panel_use

// Dismiss the card without taking anything. Same panel, opposite
// answer; N is unbound in both engine bind lists.
addmenukey "In-World Panel: Dismiss Card" rs_panel_dismiss
alias rs_panel_dismiss "netevent rs-panel-dismiss"
defaultbind N rs_panel_dismiss

